    globe: { cap: 'London, 1599', ll: [-0.097, 51.508], tx: 'Arts Figures get +1 Might here.', fact: 'A cannon fired during a play set its thatched roof alight and burned it down in 1613.', art: 'The Globe Theatre in London, a round timber playhouse with a thatched roof seen from the crowded yard, players on the stage, flags flying.' },
    machu: { cap: 'Peru, c. 1450', ll: [-72.55, -13.16], tx: 'Only the player whose side it is on can walk Figures in.', fact: 'The Inca built it high in the Andes without mortar, wheels or iron tools.', art: 'Machu Picchu on its ridge above the clouds, stone terraces and houses, a sharp green peak behind, a llama on a terrace.' },
    timbuktu: { cap: 'Mali, 14th century', ll: [-3.0, 16.77], tx: 'Figures arriving here cost 1 less.', fact: 'Its scholars and libraries made it one of the great centers of learning in Africa.', art: 'Timbuktu: a great mud-brick mosque with wooden beams studding its towers, scholars with books in the square, camels and traders, desert sky.' },
    petra: { cap: 'Jordan, c. 300 BC', ll: [35.44, 30.33], tx: 'Figures here can\'t be targeted by enemy Deeds.', fact: 'The Nabataeans carved its temples and tombs straight into rose-red cliffs.', art: 'Petra: a columned temple front carved into a rose-red cliff, glimpsed at the end of a narrow shadowed canyon, a rider entering.' },
    nineveh: { cap: 'Assyria, 7th century BC', ll: [43.15, 36.36], tx: 'A quiet Place with no rule of its own.', fact: 'Ashurbanipal\'s library here held tens of thousands of clay tablets.', art: 'A gate of Nineveh, capital of Assyria, about 690 BC, seen straight on from directly in front of it, close and low, in strict one-point perspective looking down the gate passage like a corridor. The passage is a deep tunnel with a round semicircular arch through a massive mud-brick city wall with stepped crenellations, and at its far end, at the vanishing point, the sunlit city shows. The two side walls of this corridor ARE a matched pair of colossal lamassu, human-headed winged bulls about four and a half metres tall (two and a half times a man\'s height), each carved from a single block of pale gypsum stone and built into the gateway as its door jambs. At the mouth of the passage, at its left corner and its right corner, the front of each bull faces straight out at us: a calm bearded human face, the tall rounded crown of a god with three pairs of horns curving up its front and a ring of feathers and a band of rosettes at the top, hair in curls to the shoulders, bull\'s ears with earrings, a long squared beard in tiers of curls, a broad chest covered in rows of curls, and two forelegs planted together. Behind each front, its long body runs straight back away from us down the inside of the passage, lining the corridor wall and steeply foreshortened: the feathered wing swept back, the flank, the striding hind legs and the wedge-shaped writing carved between the legs all recede toward the vanishing point, the left bull\'s flank forming the left wall of the corridor and the right bull\'s flank forming the right wall, mirror images of each other. The outside face of the city wall to the left and right of the gateway is completely plain brick: no statue, wing or relief on it. People give the true scale: each bull is about two and a half times the height of a man, so a robed man leading a laden donkey out through the passage, walking toward us between the two bulls in the foreground, stands as tall as the bulls\' chests, his head level with the bottom of their beards, and a spearman on guard stands beside one bull\'s foreleg at the same size. The passage between the bulls is wide enough for a loaded cart. The round arch springs from just above the bulls\' crowns. Their ears are a bull\'s ears, set at the sides of the head below the crown, and the horns lie flat against the front of the crown, curving up it, not sticking out. Low evening sun lights the two stone faces, with deep shadow inside the passage. Symmetrical. Not a pointed arch.' },
